# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Tool write-ups (description field) for Power Play. Keyed by tool media id."""
DESCRIPTIONS = {
"M1.S2.T2.C5.2D1": (
"The student starts with one sheet of paper drawn as a thin line, 0.001 cm thick, with a fold counter at 0 and a Fold button. "
"Two bars stand side by side. One bar adds 0.001 cm for every fold and the other doubles. A readout gives the thickness of the doubling stack "
"and changes its own unit from cm to m to km as the stack grows. A tall scale in kilometres runs up the side and carries a marker for the Moon at 384400 km.\n\n"
"Before each fold the student enters the thickness expected next. Pressing Fold doubles the stack and writes the previous thickness times 2 under the new one, "
"so the student sees where it came from. The bars start level and then part ways: after 2 folds the doubling bar reads 0.004 cm while the adding bar has grown by only 0.001 cm per fold. "
"The student can also jump the counter by 5 or by 10 folds and read the factor the thickness gained, then fold on until the stack climbs the scale towards the Moon marker.\n\n"
"The six cards ask: is the thickness after 7 folds 0.007 cm or 0.128 cm; how many times thicker is the stack from fold 3 to fold 6 (8 times); by what factor does it grow from fold 10 to fold 20 (1024); "
"a stack is 0.016 cm thick after 4 folds, so how thick is it after 5 folds (0.032 cm) and after 8 folds (0.256 cm); is a stack of about 351844 km after 45 folds past the Moon (no, the Moon is at 384400 km); "
"and after which fold does it first pass the Moon (fold 46, more than 700000 km). A factor found by multiplying the number of folds by 2, such as 20 for ten folds, is tested and fails when the tool doubles ten times and reaches 1024.\n\n"
"Hints come in order: what happens to the thickness at every single fold; doubling means multiplying by 2 each time, not adding the same amount; count the folds between the two stacks and multiply by 2 once for each of them; "
"and last, the folds are doubled one at a time with the thickness shown, then a new card follows. Through doing, the student learns that a stack that doubles grows by multiplying and not by adding, "
"so n folds multiply the starting thickness by 2 to the power n, any 3 folds make it 8 times as thick and any 10 folds make it 1024 times as thick."
),
"M2.S4.T4.C12.2D1": (
"Three linked panels sit side by side, the way a short form, its long form and its value are written along one line of a notebook. On the left is the power in large type with its base and exponent named, "
"in the centre a strip of factor blocks, and on the right the value. The tool opens on 2 to the power 5 with the strip and the value empty, and the student must commit to a value, 10 or 32, before anything is drawn.\n\n"
"Pressing Expand lays five blocks on the strip, each carrying a 2, and the student steps the product one block at a time: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, with each partial value held under the block that made it and the student's guess kept beside the result. "
"The student then drags the exponent up or down. Each change adds or removes exactly one block and the value is multiplied or divided by the base once, so one more in the exponent reads as one more factor and not one more unit. "
"Changing the base, from 1 to 10, redraws every block but leaves the block count where it was. Typing a value into the value panel makes the tool look for the exponent that gives it at the current base, and refuses a value that no exponent gives. "
"A typed value that disagrees with the blocks makes the product replay from the first block with the student's number pinned above it, so the point where it left the sequence is visible. "
"A long strip folds into its first few blocks, the count and the last block, and values are always printed in full.\n\n"
"Four hunts use the tower. Find a base and exponent where base times exponent equals the power, and see that only an exponent of 1 works for every base, plus the single case 2 squared. "
"Find the base whose tower never grows however tall it is built, which is 1. Swap the base and exponent of a power and say which is larger, since 2 to the power 10 is 1024 but 10 to the power 2 is 100. "
"Or build any power, predict its value and expand to check. An exponent of 0 leaves the strip empty and the value reads 1, the product of no factors, with a note that the chapter explains why later. "
"If the student says the exponent multiplies the base, the answer is rejected with the blocks pointed at. In practice items the student expands and evaluates powers, judges claims such as 5 to the power 3 is 15, and says what the exponent counts. "
"A wrong answer replays the product with the student's value beside the true one.\n\n"
"Through doing, the student learns that a power counts how many times the base is used as a factor, that the exponent never multiplies the base, and that one more in the exponent multiplies the value by the base once more."
),
"M3.S6.T6.C23.2D1": (
"Two strips of identical blocks lie on a plain ground with a visible gap between them. Strip A holds two blocks of 2 and strip B holds three blocks of 2, and each strip has a bracket with its own count and its own colour. "
"The single power form is hidden, and the student is asked how many 2s there will be once the strips are joined.\n\n"
"The student drags strip A onto strip B. The gap closes and the row counts itself off from 1 to 5, the first two numerals in strip A's colour and the last three in strip B's, so a student who counts the last block of A again sees the slip. "
"Under the row the expanded product is written, and under that the single power, 2 to the power 5. Two sliders set the lengths of A and B from 0 to 6, and a strip with no blocks can be joined: it adds nothing, so the row does not change. "
"The student can regroup the seven 3s of the king's story as four and three or as two and five and always reach 3 to the power 7, which is 2187. A Letters switch turns the blocks into p, so four p's joined to six p's give p to the power 10.\n\n"
"Strips of different bases will not join. Three 2s and two 5s spring apart, and the tool says that a row can be written as one power only when every block carries the same number, and shows that 8 times 25 is 200, which is not a power of 2. "
"A rule box lets the student type their own rule for the joined count. The tool runs it on three pairs the student has not seen and names the ones that break. "
"The rule 'multiply the counts' fits 2 and 2, but run at 2 and 3 it puts 64 beside the 32 of the counted row, and it fails again at 3 and 1 and at 4 and 2. "
"Practice items give strips such as 4 threes and 2 threes, three strips of 2, 3 and 4 twos that fuse into 2 to the power 9, claims that the exponents multiply, and the question of why a joined row is never shorter than either strip. "
"A wrong answer replays the join with the student's exponent beside the true one.\n\n"
"Through doing, the student learns that the exponents add because the blocks are counted, that the rule works only when the bases match, and that a rule which fits one case is not yet a rule."
),
"M3.S8.T8.C33.2D1": (
"A bench carries two rows of blocks with different bases: three blocks of 2 in the top row and three blocks of 5 in the bottom row, with empty pair slots between them. "
"The student reads the base and the count of each row and predicts how many pairs can be made before touching anything.\n\n"
"The student drags one block from each row into a pair slot, and the pair snaps together into a single block showing the product of its two bases, 2 times 5, which is 10. Every pair is the same new block. "
"After three pairs the bench holds three blocks of 10, read as 10 to the power 3, and its value 1000 is checked against 8 times 125. A second setting turns the bench into a quotient. "
"Three blocks of 12 sit over three blocks of 4, and each pair makes a block showing 12 divided by 4, which is 3, so the three pairs give 3 to the power 3, which is 27. The exponent counts the pairs, so it is not divided.\n\n"
"The six cards are 2 to the power 3 with 5 to the power 3 (10 cubed, 1000); 3 to the power 4 times 4 to the power 4 (12 to the power 4, 20736); 2 to the power 5 times 3 to the power 5 (6 to the power 5, 7776); "
"12 cubed divided by 4 cubed (3 cubed, 27); 15 squared divided by 3 squared (5 squared, 25); and 2 to the power 4 times 3 to the power 5, where four pairs of 6 can be made and one 3 is left over. "
"Rows with different counts leave blocks unpaired, and the tool shows them outside the pairs; asked to write such a product as one power, it refuses and gives the reason. "
"A rule that multiplies the exponents is tested on 2 squared times 3 squared: it predicts 6 to the power 4, which is 1296, while the pairs give 6 squared, which is 36.\n\n"
"Hints come in order: how many blocks are in each row, and are the counts equal; each pair holds one block of each base and every pair is the same new block; multiply the two bases for one pair, then count the pairs to get the exponent; "
"and last, the pairs are made and written as one power with its value, then a new card follows. Through doing, the student learns that powers with the same exponent can be regrouped in pairs, "
"so m to the power a times n to the power a is (m times n) to the power a, and m to the power a divided by n to the power a is (m divided by n) to the power a."
),
"M3.S10.T10.C41.2D1": (
"A lock fills the middle of the screen with a row of slots, and under it a tree of choices opens one slot at a time with a running count of codes beside each slot. "
"The student sets each slot to digits (10 choices), letters A to Z (26 choices) or either (36 choices). On the first card the slots are labelled shirts, trousers and shoes and carry 5, 4 and 2 choices.\n\n"
"Before opening the next slot the student predicts how many codes there will be. Adding a slot opens another level of the tree: every branch splits into as many branches as the new slot has choices, "
"and each slot shows the count before it and the count after it, so with digit slots the student watches 10, then 100, then 1000. The first levels are drawn in full and later ones fold into a branch that carries its count. "
"Slots with the same number of choices are grouped and written as one power, so four digit slots read 10 to the power 4 and three letter slots read 26 to the power 3. "
"A student who adds the numbers of choices sees that total drawn beside the tree, 5 plus 4 plus 2 is 11 against 40 outfits, and sees how much smaller it is.\n\n"
"The six cards are: 5 shirts, 4 trousers and 2 pairs of shoes (40 outfits); 4 digit slots, written as a power of 10 (10 to the power 4, 10000 codes); 3 letter slots A to Z (26 to the power 3, 17576 codes); "
"2 digit slots and 2 letter slots (10 squared times 26 squared, 67600 codes); 3 slots that each take a letter or a digit (36 to the power 3, 46656 codes); "
"and a 5-digit lock that gets one more digit slot, where the number of codes grows by a factor of 10. "
"Hints come in order: how many choices does each slot have; each choice in one slot can go with every choice in the next slot, so the counts multiply; multiply the choices slot by slot and write equal slots as a power; "
"and last, the tree is opened and the codes counted, then a new card follows.\n\n"
"Through doing, the student learns that each slot multiplies the count of codes by its number of choices, that equal slots make a power, and that n slots of 10 digits give 10 to the power n codes."
),
"M4.S12.T12.C49.2D1": (
"A fraction bar runs across the middle of the screen with a row of factor blocks above it and a row below, both rows drawn from the same left edge so each block above sits over a block below. "
"The tool opens with five blocks of 3 above the bar and two below, and a value readout under it shows 243 over 9, which is 27. "
"The number of 3s that survive and the single power form stay hidden until the student predicts how many 3s will be left above the bar.\n\n"
"The student taps a block above the bar and then a block below it. Both grey out as a pair, each is redrawn as a 1 rather than removed, and a dashed link joins them. "
"A cancelled pair divides to 1, and the value readout pulses once but never changes, so cancelling is seen as dividing and not as deleting. Undo returns a pair and the value again does not move. "
"After two pairs the bar holds three 3s above, written 3 to the power 3, which is 27. Two sliders set the counts above and below, each from 0 to 8. "
"When the counts are equal everything cancels and the bar is left holding 1 over 1, and the tool asks what that is worth before it writes anything. "
"With 2 twos above and 5 below, two pairs cancel and three 2s stay below the bar, an exact fraction, 1 over 8, never a decimal. With nothing below the bar no pair can form and the value is unchanged. "
"Blocks of different bases refuse to cancel and say why.\n\n"
"Checks the student meets: 4 threes over 4 threes is 1 and not 0; a student who writes 3 to the power 6 over 3 to the power 2 as 3 to the power 3 by dividing the exponents is shown six 3s over two, "
"two pairs cancelled and four 3s left, which is 81; and 2 to the power 5 over 2 to the power 2 reads 32 over 4 before cancelling and 8 after. "
"Practice items ask for the surviving exponent, the value of a fully cancelled fraction, the value of the surviving power, and why the exponents subtract. "
"A wrong answer replays the cancelling with the student's answer beside the true one.\n\n"
"Through doing, the student learns that a to the power m divided by a to the power n is a to the power m minus n for a base that is not 0, because each cancelled pair divides to 1 and removes one factor from each row. "
"The leftovers below the bar when n is the larger count are where negative exponents begin."
),
"M4.S14.T14.C57.2D1": (
"A staircase of value cards descends to the right, reading 16, 8, 4, 2 for the powers of 2 from the fourth down to the first, with the exponent printed beside each step. "
"Between every pair of cards an arrow reads divide by 2, and below the last card is an empty, outlined slot. The student is asked what comes next and what 2 to the power 0 will be before the slot is filled.\n\n"
"The student drags the next step into its slot, and its arrow draws itself first, so the rule is on screen before any number is asked for. The student types the value, and the tool checks it against the previous step divided by the base. "
"A value that breaks the rule is refused with the arrow highlighted and the correct number is not given, so the pattern does the teaching. The step lands on 1, because it is the base divided by itself. "
"The base can be changed, from 2 to 10 and also to 1 or 0 as test cases, and the whole staircase rebuilds with new values while the landing stays at 1.Before base 7 is drawn the student predicts whether it too will land on 1, and a record shows which bases have been tried. "
"Base 1 gives a flat staircase of ones that still lands on 1. Base 0 is blocked with the reason that dividing by 0 has no next step, so 0 to the power 0 is not defined here. "
"The staircase can be continued below the exponent 0 into the fractions 1 over 2, 1 over 4 and so on, so the pattern is seen not to stop.\n\n"
"Practice items give staircases of other bases and ask for the next step down, for example 243, 81, 27 at base 3 and 343, 49, 7 at base 7; ask what the step at the exponent 0 reads and why; "
"evaluate products such as 9 to the power 0 times 2 to the power 3, which is 8; judge claims such as a zero exponent takes the number away; and add all the steps of the base 2 staircase from the exponent 4 down to 0, which is 31. "
"A wrong answer replays the descent with the student's value beside the true one.\n\n"
"Through doing, the student learns that every step down divides by the base because it removes one factor, that a power with exponent 0 is 1 for any base that is not 0, and that the argument never depends on which base is used."
),
"M4.S16.T16.C64.2D1": (
"A horizontal power line for base 4 runs across the screen. It has a labelled stop for every power from 4 to the power minus 4 on the left to 4 to the power 7 on the right, "
"each stop showing the power and its value, for example 4 cubed with 64 and 4 to the power minus 2 with 1 over 16. A token stands on one stop. Under the line sit move cards that say multiply by or divide by a power of the base.\n\n"
"The student reads the stop the token is on and the move card, predicts the stop where the token will land, and then plays the card. The token hops along the line one stop at a time with the hops counted, "
"up for multiplying and down for dividing, and the landing is read as a power and as a number. A move down that passes 1 lands on a fraction stop, shown as 1 over a power. "
"A landing worked out by multiplying the exponents is tested and shown to miss: 4 cubed times 4 squared would be sent to 4 to the power 6, while the token lands on 4 to the power 5, which is 1024. "
"The student can check a landing by working out both numbers and dividing. The line can be rebuilt for another base, such as 3.\n\n"
"The six cards are: start at 4 cubed and multiply by 4 squared (4 to the power 5); start at 4 to the power 6 and divide by 4 to the power 4 (4 squared, 16); "
"start at 4 squared and divide by 4 to the power 5 (4 to the power minus 3, 1 over 64); on the power line of 3, start at 3 to the power 5 and divide by 3 to the power 7 (3 to the power minus 2, 1 over 9); "
"how many times larger is 4 to the power 7 than 4 cubed (four stops apart, so 4 to the power 4, which is 256 times); and how many times larger is 4 than 4 to the power minus 2 (three stops apart, 64 times). "
"Hints come in order: which stop does the token start on, and does the card multiply or divide; multiplying by a power moves the token up that many steps and dividing moves it down; "
"count the steps from the exponent on the card and move the token that far; and last, the token is moved to the right stop and the power is read, then a new card follows.\n\n"
"Through doing, the student learns that every step up the line multiplies by the base and every step down divides by it, so multiplying powers adds exponents, dividing subtracts them, "
"and the gap between two stops tells how many times larger one number is than the other."
),
"M5.S18.T18.C72.2D1": (
"Two power cards are dealt face up side by side, 2 to the power 10 on the left and 10 to the power 3 on the right, each in large type with a blank value panel under it. "
"Below the cards is a list of reasons for choosing one card, and a scoreboard along the side records which reasons the student has used. Both values stay hidden until a reason has been submitted.\n\n"
"The student taps the card they think is larger, and it lifts and is held while nothing is marked. Then the student picks a reason from the list: same base, so the larger exponent wins; same exponent, so the larger base wins; "
"rewrite a card onto a common base or exponent; or neither is shared, so bound each between neighbouring powers of 10 and then work out the values. A reason that does not fit the pair is named as not applying, "
"and the field it would need is pointed at. A reason that fits is applied on the cards. For 4 to the power 4 against 2 to the power 8, the 4 becomes 2 squared, so the first card becomes 2 squared raised to the power 4, "
"the exponents multiply to 8, and both cards read 2 to the power 8, a dead heat, which the tool allows as an answer. For the opening pair no rewrite exists because 10 is not a power of 2, and both cards lie between 10 cubed and 10 to the power 4, "
"so only the values settle it. Reveal fills both value panels at the same instant, 1024 against 1000.\n\n"
"Pairs the student meets include 2 to the power 7 against 5 to the power 3 (128 against 125, the smaller base wins); 3 to the power 5 against 5 to the power 3 (243 against 125, only working the values settles it); "
"9 to the power 3 against 3 to the power 7, where 9 is 3 squared so the first card is 3 to the power 6 and the bigger base loses; and 2 to the power 5 against 5 squared, where the pick is right but the reason that 5 is the bigger exponent is not, "
"because 3 to the power 4 beats 2 to the power 5. A right pick with an invalid reason is scored as partly right and a hint follows. Cards in standard form are dealt too, such as 1.4335 times 10 to the power 12 against 1.496 times 10 to the power 11, "
"where the exponents are compared first. Points come from reasons and never from speed, and no timer is shown. Deal a new pair locks the reveal again.\n\n"
"Through doing, the student learns that a bigger base alone decides nothing, that two powers are compared by putting them on a common base or exponent where one exists, and that the reason must come before the arithmetic."
),
"M6.S22.T22.C91.2D1": (
"Two numbers in scientific notation stand on the left, each split into a coefficient block and a power of 10 block, for example 4 times 10 to the power 6 and 5 times 10 to the power 3. "
"On the right is a result card with a decimal point that the student can slide, and an exponent counter that moves the opposite way as the point slides. "
"The student reads the coefficient and the exponent of each number and predicts the result before standard form is reached.\n\n"
"The student combines the numbers on the result card: for a product the coefficients are multiplied and the exponents added, giving 20 times 10 to the power 9, and for a quotient the coefficients are divided and the exponents subtracted. "
"A coefficient outside 1 to 10 is marked as not yet in standard form. The student slides the point until the coefficient is in range. One place left makes 20 into 2.0 and the exponent counter rises from 9 to 10, so the number is 2 times 10 to the power 10, "
"and one place right lowers the exponent by one. The size of the number never changes, only the way it is written. A result judged by its coefficient alone is tested against the exponents and shown to fail.\n\n"
"The six cards are: 2 times 10 to the power 16 divided by 8 times 10 to the power 9, the ants for every person (0.25 times 10 to the power 7, then 2.5 times 10 to the power 6); "
"4 times 10 to the power 6 times 5 times 10 to the power 3 (2 times 10 to the power 10); 6 times 10 to the power 8 divided by 2 times 10 to the power 3 (3 times 10 to the power 5, already in standard form, so no slide is needed); "
"7 times 10 to the power 4 times 9 times 10 to the power 5 (63 times 10 to the power 9, then 6.3 times 10 to the power 10); 2 times 10 to the power 5 divided by 8 times 10 to the power 9 (0.25 times 10 to the power minus 4, then 2.5 times 10 to the power minus 5); "
"and 1.5 times 10 to the power 7 times 3.15 times 10 to the power 7 (4.725 times 10 to the power 14). "
"Hints come in order: what are the coefficient and the exponent of each number; multiply or divide the coefficients, and add or subtract the exponents; slide the decimal point until the coefficient is at least 1 and less than 10, and change the exponent to match; "
"and last, the result is put in standard form with its exponent, then a new card follows.\n\n"
"Through doing, the student learns to combine the coefficients and the exponents of 10 separately, and to slide the point and change the exponent together, so the coefficient ends at least 1 and less than 10 while the number stays the same."
),
}
